<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">York</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Eboracum, Jorvik</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">York is a historic city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-yorkshire">North Yorkshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, where the Ouse and the Foss meet. Founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in 71 CE as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eboracum">Eboracum</a>, it was where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a> was proclaimed emperor in 306. It became the seat of an archbishop and capital of Northumbria, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> kingdom of Jorvik from 866, and a leading city of medieval England.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/york.jpg" alt="York" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: FEGreene · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:York_Minster_(2797690).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Northumbria, the land of the people north of the Humber, was formed from two Anglian kingdoms: Bernicia, centred on the coastal fortress of Bamburgh, whose dynasty traced its descent from Ida in the mid 6th century, and Deira, centred on the Yorkshire Wolds and <b>York</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Most people lived in farmsteads and villages much like those of the Iron Age, but a network of towns grew up: coloniae of veterans at Colchester, Lincoln and Gloucester, later <b>York</b>, and civitas capitals for the native peoples, such as Verulamium (St Albans), Corinium (Cirencester) and Viroconium (Wroxeter).</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>For centuries Greenland Inuit also took iron from the Cape <b>York</b> meteorite, cold-forging slices of it with stones into tools.</blockquote><a href="/culture/inuit">Inuit · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>Long before European contact the Thule worked iron, some of it from meteorites such as the Cape <b>York</b> meteorite in Greenland and some traded from the Norse.</blockquote><a href="/culture/thule-people">Thule people · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Edwin, king of Deira and Bernicia, was baptised with his chief men by Paulinus, the first Bishop of <b>York</b>, according to Bede.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-12">12 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Dick Turpin, convicted of horse theft, was hanged on the Knavesmire at <b>York</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-7">7 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
